      "source_excerpt": "3.2.2 Statistical Methodologies: \"The primary and secondary endpoints were analyzed using the Cochran-Mantel-Haenszel (CMH)\" \"test stratified by sites.\" \"The primary endpoint was tested using one-sided significance level of\" \"0.025.\" \"Multiplicity for the two key secondary endpoints was controlled using the Hochberg’s\" \"method.\" \"For the Hochberg’s method, if the primary efficacy endpoint analysis provides a\" \"statistically significant result, then the two secondary efficacy endpoints are tested using the\" \"same method (one-sided level of significance of 0.025 using a CMH test) to show superiority of\" \"NAFT-600 over vehicle.\" \"If both p-values from the secondary endpoint analyses (one-sided) are\" \"≤0.025, both comparisons are considered statistically significant; if only one endpoint provides a\" \"p-value ≤0.025, then it must be ≤0.0125 in order to be considered statistically significant.\" \"• The proportion of subjects with complete cure of interdigital tinea pedis at Week 6 where\" \"dermatophyte culture at Week 6\" \"erythema, scaling and pruritus scores of 0 or 1 at Week 6.\" -- The schema has no Hochberg procedure enum, so procedure is null and the method is preserved in method_citations. The primary is tested one-sided at 0.025; the two key secondary endpoints are gated on primary success and use Hochberg at one-sided 0.025, with the stated 0.0125 threshold if only one secondary p-value is at most 0.025. alpha_sided=1 interprets the explicitly stated one-sided tests.",
